\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Market Settlement Decoupling and Metric Inversion Diagnostic under UK BSC Netting. (a) Reductio ad absurdum: the degenerate zero-bid settlement anomaly ($\hat  {y} \equiv 0$) incurs zero shortfall penalty and extracts over \pounds  220k/turb$\cdot $yr in apparent cashflow without generating forecasts, exposing single-settlement cashflow as an unhedged evaluation trap. (b) Empirical metric inversion on Hill of Towie, where distribution constraints increase nominal balancing cashflow by $+42.5\%$ while concurrently degrading genuine decision risk by $+6.5\%$ and RMSE by $+3.5\%$.}}{31}{figure.caption.28}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:market_decoupling}{{7}{31}{Market Settlement Decoupling and Metric Inversion Diagnostic under UK BSC Netting. (a) Reductio ad absurdum: the degenerate zero-bid settlement anomaly ($\hat {y} \equiv 0$) incurs zero shortfall penalty and extracts over \pounds 220k/turb$\cdot $yr in apparent cashflow without generating forecasts, exposing single-settlement cashflow as an unhedged evaluation trap. (b) Empirical metric inversion on Hill of Towie, where distribution constraints increase nominal balancing cashflow by $+42.5\%$ while concurrently degrading genuine decision risk by $+6.5\%$ and RMSE by $+3.5\%$}{figure.caption.28}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Empirical Contrast between Official Reverse-JS and Corrected Standard-JS across Regularization Scales $\lambda \in [0.01, 1.0]$ on Kelmarsh and Penmanshiel (Multi-seed means and standard deviations). (a) Mean gradient $L_2$ norm: the reverse-argument JS form induces substantial gradient amplification at $\lambda = 1.0$ ($2.20\times $ on Kelmarsh, $2.72\times $ on Penmanshiel) due to the $-\frac  {P_{\text  {ref}, i}}{4 P_{\text  {pred}, i}}$ inverse-density singularity ($\mathcal  {O}(1/\epsilon )$ in Equation~\eqref  {eq:reverse_js_gradient}), whereas for Corrected Standard-JS the signed partial derivative possesses a finite upper bound ($\frac  {1}{2}\ln 2$) while exhibiting only mild logarithmic divergence ($\mathcal  {O}(|\log \epsilon |)$) as $P_{\text  {pred}, i} \to 0$. (b) Operational dispatch cost ($r=2$): Corrected Standard-JS prevents premature performance degradation at weak $\lambda =0.01$, while excessive regularization degrades dispatch across both variants.}}{32}{figure.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pct_contrast}{{8}{32}{Empirical Contrast between Official Reverse-JS and Corrected Standard-JS across Regularization Scales $\lambda \in [0.01, 1.0]$ on Kelmarsh and Penmanshiel (Multi-seed means and standard deviations). (a) Mean gradient $L_2$ norm: the reverse-argument JS form induces substantial gradient amplification at $\lambda = 1.0$ ($2.20\times $ on Kelmarsh, $2.72\times $ on Penmanshiel) due to the $-\frac {P_{\text {ref}, i}}{4 P_{\text {pred}, i}}$ inverse-density singularity ($\mathcal {O}(1/\epsilon )$ in Equation~\eqref {eq:reverse_js_gradient}), whereas for Corrected Standard-JS the signed partial derivative possesses a finite upper bound ($\frac {1}{2}\ln 2$) while exhibiting only mild logarithmic divergence ($\mathcal {O}(|\log \epsilon |)$) as $P_{\text {pred}, i} \to 0$. (b) Operational dispatch cost ($r=2$): Corrected Standard-JS prevents premature performance degradation at weak $\lambda =0.01$, while excessive regularization degrades dispatch across both variants}{figure.caption.35}{}}
